  "comment:exports": "THE CANONICAL RATIONALE — every sibling's `comment:exports` says \"see @agentic-toolkit/adh for the full rationale\" and means this key. (1) `development` → src so `next dev` and vitest compile this package's TypeScript in-process: HMR on a source edit, and no `pnpm build` between editing a component here and seeing it in a consuming site. A production build (NODE_ENV=production) does not match the condition and falls through to `import` → dist, which is what ships. (2) It is SELF-DEGRADING: a bundler that ignores unknown conditions just takes `import`/dist, so the worst case is the pre-condition behavior (rebuild to see a change), never a broken resolve — which is why it is safe to carry in every package. (3) `types` stays first and always points at dist: editors and `tsc` read the built .d.ts, so a consumer's typecheck never depends on this package's src being compilable in the consumer's own tsconfig. (4) The pairing is load-bearing in BOTH directions — a src file the prod `import` condition points at but tsup never built is invisible in dev and a hard resolve failure in prod, so entry globs and this map must name the same files and extensions (an exports map has no extension fallback). verify-shared-dist-exports.py gates that link. (5) `./sources.css` is the Tailwind @source self-registration a host @imports once; see sources.css itself for why it is package-wide.",
